-- title:
--   Supersingular branch with second Drinfeld section at the origin
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime with $q\ge 5$, $M'$ is a nonzero natural number not divisible by $q$, and $\ell'$ is a prime with $\ell'\neq q$, $3\le\ell'$ and $\ell'\nmid M'$.
--
--   **The valuation subring above $q$ and its constant reduction.** $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with `A.LiesOverPrime q`, i.e. $q$ lies in the nonunits of $A$. $W$ is a finite set of places of the field $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC} (\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A)\,M'$ — the intermediate field of $\mathrm{ResidueField}(A)\subseteq$ Laurent series generated by $\mathrm{jqModC}$ and $\mathrm{jqNModC}\,M'$ — over $\mathrm{ResidueField}(A)$, and `hW` says that $W$ consists exactly of the places satisfying `IsSupersingularPlace q M'`; $s$ is a distinguished element of $W$. The hypothesis `hle` asserts the inclusion of intermediate fields $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar} M'\le \mathrm{fieldBar} q M'$, i.e. the base change to $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ of the full level-$M'$ modular function field is contained in the base change of the function field of $X_H$ of level $q^2M'$ with $H=\mathrm{levelH} q M'$ the kernel of $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/q)^\times$. Further, $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` datum for $A$ from $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar} M'$ to $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC} (\mathrm{ResidueField}\,A)\,M'$ (a valuation subring `integers` of the source, a surjective residue homomorphism with kernel the maximal ideal, a map on places preserving degrees, compatibility with $A$ and with divisors), and `hR₀` requires that for every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose coefficientwise image under the inclusion $A\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lies in $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldBar} M'$, that element lies in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ and its $R_0$-residue, read as a Laurent series over $\mathrm{ResidueField}(A)$, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$.
--
--   **The base field and the local ring.** $k_0$ is an intermediate field of $\mathbb Q\subseteq\overline{\mathbb Q}$ and $\pi_0\in k_0$ an element lying in $A$ (`hπ`). Write $A_0:=A.\mathrm{comap}$ along $k_0\to\overline{\mathbb Q}$. The hypotheses `hdvr`, `hunif`, `hhens`, `hres` state that $A_0$ is a discrete valuation ring whose maximal ideal is generated by $\pi_0$, that $A_0$ is a henselian local ring, and that its residue field is algebraically closed; `hκ` states that every $a\in A$ is congruent modulo the maximal ideal of $A$ to an element $c\in k_0$ with $c\in A$.
--
--   **Roots of unity, the function field, the $j$-coordinate.** $\xi\in k_0$ is a primitive $(q\ell')$-th root of unity (`hξ`), and `hιξ` provides a ring homomorphism $\iota:k_0\to\mathbb C$ with $\iota\xi=\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell'))$. $K$ is an intermediate field of $k_0\subseteq$ Laurent series over $k_0$, and `hK` identifies it with the base change to $k_0$ of the function field of $X_H$ of level $(q\ell')^2M'$, where $H=\mathrm{levelH} (q\ell')\,M'$ is the kernel of $(\mathbb Z/(q\ell')^2M')^\times\to(\mathbb Z/q\ell')^\times$, i.e. the units congruent to $1$ modulo $q\ell'$. There are an $A_0$-algebra structure on $K$ compatible with $k_0$, and an element $j\in K$, assumed nonzero, whose Laurent series is the image of the $q$-expansion $\mathrm{jq}$ of the modular $j$-function under $\mathrm{coeffEmb} k_0$ (`hj`). Set $C:=\mathrm{chartAlgFin} A_0\,K\,j$, the $A_0$-subalgebra of $K$ attached to $j$ in the two-chart integral model, with distinguished element $\mathrm{jChartFin} A_0\,K\,j$ given by $j$.
--
--   **Functoriality and group-law data.** `hℓ` and `hM` state that, over every $A_0$-algebra $T$, level-$\ell'$ structures (in the sense of `IsLevelPStructure`) and $\Gamma_0(p^k)$-kernel polynomials (in the sense of `IsGamma0PowAt`) transport along Weierstrass variable changes, by $D\mapsto D.\mathrm{variableChange}\,C$ and by `kernelVariableChangeDeg` with degree `gamma0PowDeg p k` respectively. $\mathcal G$ is a family `GroupLaws A₀` of relative group laws on projective Weierstrass models with unit discriminant, assumed chord-and-tangent (`h𝒢`) and with the origin as identity section (`h𝒢O`); $\mathcal T$ is a `LevelTransport A₀ 𝒢 q` datum, assumed to transport sections (`h𝒯`). The hypotheses `hVC` and `hCO` supply, for every projective Weierstrass curve over an $A_0$-algebra, graded ring homomorphisms between the graded rings of the projective models realising a variable change (`IsVariableChangeHom`) and a coefficient change along an $A_0$-algebra map (`IsCoefficientHom`), each together with the inclusion of irrelevant ideals required for the induced map of $\mathrm{Proj}$'s. Let $\mathcal R:=\mathrm{rigidDataPow} A_0\,\ell'\,M'\,q\,h\ell\,hM\,\mathcal G\,\mathcal T$ be the resulting rigid Weierstrass data, the rigidification of the product of the $\Gamma_0(p^k)$-kernel component at $M'$, the level-$\ell'$ component and the Drinfeld level-$q$ component; a raw point of $\mathcal R$ over $T$ consists of a Weierstrass curve with unit discriminant together with level data, and the points $\mathcal R.\mathrm{Pt}\,T$ are raw points modulo variable change.
--
--   **The moduli package and the Tate normalisation.** $P_0$ is a `LevelModuliPackageAbs` for $A_0$ and $\mathcal R.\mathrm{toLevelModuliDatum}$, that is, an $A_0$-algebra $B_0$ with a universal point such that every point over an $A_0$-algebra is the image of the universal one under a unique $A_0$-algebra map. $x_0$ is a point of the datum over $K$ whose $j$-invariant, as a Laurent series over $k_0$, is $\mathrm{jqNModC} k_0\,(q\ell')$ (`hx₀`), and $clC$ is an $A_0$-algebra map $B_0\to C$ which, composed with the inclusion $C\subseteq K$, agrees with $P_0.\mathrm{classify}\,x_0$ (`hclC`). The hypothesis `hμ` pins $x_0$ down at the cusp: there are a variable change $C_\flat$ over the Laurent series over $k_0$ and a raw point $r$ of $\mathcal R$ over $K$ with class $x_0$, such that (i) with $\zeta$ the unit determined by $\xi$, the unit $C_\flat.u$ satisfies $C_\flat.u\cdot\bigl(2\,(\mathrm{cuspPoint} k_0\,(q\ell')\,\zeta\,(1,0))_1+\mathrm C(6^{-1})\bigr)=2\,(\mathrm{cuspPoint} k_0\,(q\ell')\,\zeta\,(1,0))_2+(\mathrm{cuspPoint} k_0\,(q\ell')\,\zeta\,(1,0))_1$, while $C_\flat.r=\mathrm C(-12^{-1})$, $C_\flat.s=\mathrm C(-2^{-1})$, $C_\flat.t=\mathrm C(24^{-1})$; (ii) the curve of $r$, pushed to the Laurent series over $k_0$, equals $C_\flat\bullet\mathrm{tateBase} k_0\,(q\ell')$; (iii) for every prime $p$ dividing $M'$, every field $F'$, every ring homomorphism $f:k_0\to F'$ and every primitive $p^{v_p(M')}$-th root of unity $\zeta'\in F'$, the $\Gamma_0$-power component $r.\mathrm{level}.1\,p$, pushed to the Laurent series over $k_0$ and then mapped coefficientwise by $f$, equals `kernelVariableChangeDeg` applied to the coefficientwise image of $C_\flat$, to the degree `gamma0PowDeg p (M'.factorization p)` and to $\prod_{a}\bigl(X-(\mathrm{toricPoint} F'\,(q\ell')\,\zeta'^a)_1\bigr)$, the product over $1\le a\le p^{v_p(M')}/2$ with $p\nmid a$; (iv) the level-$\ell'$ datum $r.\mathrm{level}.2.1$, pushed to the Laurent series over $k_0$, is the variable change by $C_\flat$ of $\mathrm{cuspData} k_0\,(q\ell')\,\zeta\,(q,0)\,(0,-q)$; and (v) there are $P_x,P_y,Q_x,Q_y\in K$ whose Laurent series are the four coordinates $x_P,y_P,x_Q,y_Q$ of the variable change by $C_\flat$ of $\mathrm{cuspData} k_0\,(q\ell')\,\zeta\,(\ell',0)\,(0,-\ell')$, and the two Drinfeld sections of $r.\mathrm{level}.2.2$ pass through these points in the sense of `IsSectionThrough`.
--
--   **The residue field comparison.** $\theta:\mathrm{ResidueField}(A_0)\to\mathrm{ResidueField}(A)$ is a ring homomorphism compatible with the two residue maps (`hθ`).
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under these hypotheses the following holds. Let $y$ be a maximal ideal of $C$, let $\psi:C\to\mathrm{ResidueField}(A_0)$ be an $A_0$-algebra map with $\psi c=0$ if and only if $c\in y$, and let $x$ be a raw point of $\mathcal R$ over $\mathrm{ResidueField}(A_0)$ whose class equals the image of the universal point $P_0.\mathrm{univ}$ under $\psi\circ clC$, and assume that the only point $P$ of the affine curve $x.\mathrm{curve}$ with $q\cdot P=0$ is $P=0$. Then there exist a type $\Omega$ with a field structure and an algebra structure over $\mathrm{ResidueField}(A)$, a place $V$ of $\Omega$ over $\mathrm{ResidueField}(A)$, a ring homomorphism $\mathrm{res}:\mathrm{ResidueField}(\mathcal O_V)\to\mathrm{ResidueField}(A)$ and a ring homomorphism $\rho:C\to\mathcal O_V$, where $\mathcal O_V$ is the valuation subring of $V$, such that:
--
--   1. $\mathrm{res}$ splits the structure map: $\mathrm{res}$ of the image of $c$ in $\mathrm{ResidueField}(\mathcal O_V)$ is $c$, for all $c\in\mathrm{ResidueField}(A)$;
--
--   2. for all $a\in A_0$, the image in $\Omega$ of $\rho$ applied to the structure image of $a$ in $C$ equals the image in $\Omega$ of $\theta$ of the residue of $a$;
--
--   3. for all $c\in C$, $\mathrm{res}$ of the residue of $\rho c$ equals $\theta(\psi c)$;
--
--   4. the image in $\Omega$ of $\rho(\mathrm{jChartFin} A_0\,K\,j)$ is transcendental over $\mathrm{ResidueField}(A)$;
--
--   5. $\Omega$ is finite-dimensional over the subfield of $\Omega$ generated over $\mathrm{ResidueField}(A)$ by that image;
--
--   6. there are an $A_0$-algebra structure on $\mathcal O_V$ and an $A_0$-algebra map $\rho_A:B_0\to\mathcal O_V$ with $\rho_A b=\rho(clC\,b)$ for all $b\in B_0$, and a raw point $x_V$ of $\mathcal R$ over $\mathcal O_V$ whose class is the image of $P_0.\mathrm{univ}$ under $\rho_A$, together with a ring homomorphism $\chi_Q$ from the origin-chart ring of the curve of $x_V.\mathrm{level}.2.2$ to $\Omega$ such that $\mathrm{Spec}$ of the inclusion $\mathcal O_V\to\Omega$ followed by the second Drinfeld section $Q$ of $x_V.\mathrm{level}.2.2$ equals $\mathrm{Spec}$ of $\chi_Q$ followed by the origin-chart immersion `originChartι`, and $\chi_Q(\mathrm{xOverY})=\chi_Q(\mathrm{zOverY})=0$;
--
--   7. and, in addition, there are $\gamma\in\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ with $\gamma\in\Gamma(\ell')$, $\gamma\in\Gamma_0(M')$ and both diagonal entries $\gamma_{00},\gamma_{11}$ congruent to $0$ modulo $q$, and a $k_0$-algebra automorphism $\tau$ of $K$ satisfying $\mathrm{IsLevelAutAt} k_0\,(q\ell')\,\xi\,(q\ell')\,((q\ell')^2M')\,(\mathrm{levelH}(q\ell')M')\,\gamma^{-1}\,K\,\tau$ — namely, for every weight $k$, every pair of modular forms $f,g$ of level $\Gamma_H((q\ell')^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions $p_f,p_g$ such that the rational series of $p_g$ is nonzero, every $x\in K$ whose Laurent series is the image under $\mathrm{coeffEmb}$ of the quotient of the rational series of $p_f$ by that of $p_g$, and every ring homomorphism $\iota:k_0\to\mathbb C$ with $\iota\xi=\exp(2\pi i/(q\ell'))$, the coefficientwise image under $\iota$ of $\tau x$ multiplied by the $q$-expansion of $g\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}(q\ell')\,\gamma^{-1}$ equals the $q$-expansion of $f\mid_k \mathrm{conjElemN}(q\ell')\,\gamma^{-1}$ — such that $\tau$ maps $C$ into $C$, and such that for every $h\in C$: if for every integer $n$ the $n$-th Laurent coefficient of $\tau h$ is the image in $k_0$ of an element of the maximal ideal of $A_0$, then the image of $\rho h$ in $\Omega$ is $0$.
--
--   This is the local analysis of the integral model of the full-level modular curve of level $(q\ell')^2M'$ at a supersingular point above $q$: from a maximal ideal of the chart algebra whose fibre has no nonzero $q$-torsion, it produces a place of an extension of the residue function field lying on a branch through that point on which the second Drinfeld section specialises to the origin, and it exports the level automorphism $\tau$ (attached to an anti-diagonal $\gamma\in\Gamma(\ell')\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ modulo $q$) by which the branch is cut out, in the form of a vanishing criterion for $\rho$. It feeds the construction of moduli places compatible with Frobenius in [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_isModuliPlaceOf_map_frobenius_of_forall_evalAt_eq_of_eq_map_classify_rigidDataPow_of_tatePoint`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.exists_isModuliPlaceOf_map_frobenius_of_forall_evalAt_eq_of_eq_map_classify_rigidDataPow_of_tatePoint).
